===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*Hrew-|*h₂rey-|*kret-|id2=shake|*rewdʰ-}}
The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|ridden|t=to remove debris, obstructions, etc., from (a place), to clear; to complete (something unfinished); to free (someone or something); to relieve (someone); etc.}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=ridden|pos=v|id=MED37456}}</ref> probably from {{inh|en|ang|*ryddan|t=to remove debris, obstructions, etc., from (a place), to clear}} (the {{glossary|past participle}} form {{m|ang|ġeryd}} is attested), from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*riudijaną|t=to clear}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*rewdʰ-|t=to clear land}}, or possibly from {{m|ine-pro|*Hrew-|t=to dig out, tear out; to open; to acquire}}.<ref name="OED">Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=3080387792|date=December 2024|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v|us=1}}</ref> The verb was probably also influenced by the following:<ref name="OED"/>

* {{inh|en|enm|redden|t=to rescue (someone), deliver, save; to rid (someone) of a burden; to free (someone)}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=redden|pos=v|id=MED36289}}</ref> (from which {{m|en|redd}} {{qualifier|obsolete except Northern England|Scotland}} is partly derived), from {{inh|en|ang|hreddan|t=to deliver, rescue, save; to free; to recover; to take away}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*hraddjan|t=to rescue, save}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*hradjaną|t=to rescue, save; to free; to loosen}}, possibly ultimately from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*kret-|t=to move quickly; to rattle; to shake}}; and
* {{inh|en|enm|reden|t=to arrange; to get ready, prepare; to put in order, tidy; to remove debris, obstructions, etc., from (a place), to clear; to unblock; etc.}} (whence {{m|en|rede}} {{qualifier|chiefly Scotland|archaic}}),<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=rēden|pos=''v.''(1)|noformat=1|id=MED36308}}</ref> from {{inh|en|ang|rǣdan}}, Early {{cog|ang|rēdan}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=rede|pos=''v.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=6426238110|date=September 2024}}</ref> from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*raidijan|t=to arrange}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*raidijaną|t=to arrange}}, ultimately from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*h₂rey-|t=to arrange; to count, count out}}.

The {{glossary|adjective}} is derived from {{m|en||rid}}, the past participle of the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''adj.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=9546689427|date=July 2023}}</ref> The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=6818199672|date=July 2023}}</ref>

As regards noun {{senseno|en|progress}} (“progress which has been made; speed”), compare verb {{senseno|en|complete work}} (“to complete or get through (a task, or work)”).<ref name="OED"/>

