##* {{RQ:Defoe Devil|chapter=Of [[w:God in Christianity|God]]'s Calling a Church out of the Midst of a Degenerate World, and of Satan's New Measures upon that Incident: How He Attack'd Them Immediately, and His Success in Those Attacks|page=186|passage=Thus {{w|Satan}} '''ridded''' his Hands of ten of the tvvelve Tribes {{quote-gloss|of Israel}}; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Swift Works|volume=XII|year=1727|month=September|title=To Mrs. Howard|page=257|passage=I am therefore setting out for Ireland; and it is one comfort to me, that I am '''ridding''' you of a troublesome companion.}}
##* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|volume=II|book=V|chapter=In which is Seen a More Moving Spectacle, than All the Blood in the Bodies of Thwackum and Blifil and of Twenty Other Such, is Capable of Producing|page=219|passage=[I]f you put the Lavvs in Execution, as you ought to do, you vvould ſoon '''rid''' the Country of theſe Vermin.}}
##* {{RQ:Idler|issue=54|page=1|column=3|passage=By her old friends ſhe is no longer very vvilling to be ſeen, but ſhe muſt not '''rid''' herſelf of them all at once; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Trollope Belton Estate|volume=II|chapter=Passionate Pleading|page=289|passage=On each of these days he saw Colonel Askerton, whom he found to be a civil pleasant man, willing enough to '''rid''' himself of the unpleasant task he had undertaken, but at the same time, willing also to continue his services if any further services were required of him.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1872|author=Timothy D. Sullivan|chapter=From the Accession of Henry, in 1154, to His Death in 1189|title=The Story of England. A Narrative of English History, from the Earliest Period to the Present Time.{{nb...|In Two Volumes.}}|location=Dublin|publisher=A. M. Sullivan|volume=!I (From the landing of Julius Cæsar to the Reign of Henry VIII)|page=116|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/storyofenglandna00sull/page/116/mode/1up|oclc=219827791|passage="A curse on the cowardly varlets I have about me," said the King {{quote-gloss|{{w|Henry II of England}}}}, "who have left me so long exposed to his {{quote-gloss|{{w|Thomas Becket}}'s}} insolence. Will no one '''rid''' me of this turbulent priest?"|footer={{small|The quotation attributed to Henry II was first phrased in this way in 1740, but is said to be an inaccurate statement of what the king actually said: see {{cite-book|author=w:Simon Schama|title=[[w:A History of Britain (book series)|A History of Britain: At the Edge of the World? 3000 BC&nbsp;– AD 1603]]|location=London|publisher=w:BBC Books|year=2000|page=147|isbn=978-0-563-38497-7}}.}}}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1964|month=May|title=News and Comment: Minister Hamstrings BR Workshops|journal=w:Modern Railways|location=Shepperton, Surrey|publisher=w:Ian Allan Publishing|page=291|issn=0026-8356|oclc=884584817|passage=If the Government believes that part of the railways' salvation is to be found in '''ridding''' them of extraneous concerns, it should have had the courage either to close the railway works down as quickly as possible, or to hive them off as an entirely separate concern, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=9 March 2014|author=Jacob Steinberg|title=[[w:Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan]] shock [[w:Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] in FA Cup again to reach semi-finals|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230608064158/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/mar/09/manchester-city-wigan-athletic-fa-cup|passage=All the billions in the world and Manchester City still cannot '''rid''' themselves of the most persistent thorn in their side.}}
