##* {{RQ:Turberville Faulconrie|author=Vicentino|chapter=A Treatise and Briefe Discourse, of the Cure of Spanels, when They be any Way Ouerheatte:{{nb...|Deuised and Written by Master Francesco Sforzino Vicentino the Italian, Gentleman Falconer.}}|page=366|passage=The waye to '''rydde''' this vyle diſeaſe and miſchiefe, is to beſtowe vpon the infected place, a medicine made of Gumme ''Dragaganthe'', foure ownces, infuſed in the ſtrongeſt Uyneger that may bee gotten, by the ſpace of eyght dayes: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Middleton Dekker Roaring Girle|sig=&#91;H4&#93;|verso=1|page=63|passage=[G]old he vvill giue her, / Theſe vvill I make induction to her ruine, / And '''rid''' ſhame from my houſe, griefe from my heart {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Defoe Captain Singleton|page=331|passage=[I]ndeed I began ſincerely to hate my ſelf for a Dog, a VVretch that had been a Thief, and a Murtherer; {{...}} I vvent about vvith my Heart full of theſe Thoughts, little better than a diſtracted Fellovv; in ſhort, running headlong into the dreadfulleſt Deſpair, and premeditated nothing but hovv to '''rid''' my ſelf out of the VVorld; {{...}} nothing lay upon my Mind for ſeveral Days, but to ſhoot my ſelf into the Head vvith my Piſtol.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|date=24 March 2010|author=John H. Goodwin|chapter=41|title=The Reluctant Spy|location=Bloomington, Ind.|publisher=w:AuthorHouse|page=293|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=na3hhx_n_M0C&pg=PA293|isbn=978-1-4343-8193-4|passage=Worst of all though were the leeches. The soldiers had managed to '''rid''' them from the camp interiors, but once you ventured out on patrol and into the wetlands, they were everywhere.}}
